What is a Closure in JavaScript?
👉 One-line definition (interview-ready)

A closure is a function that remembers variables from its outer scope
even after the outer function has finished executing.

(A closure is formed when a function retains access to its lexical scope (outer variables) even after the outer function has finished execution.)

What actually makes a closure?
A closure requires three things:
1️⃣ Inner function
2️⃣ Outer function variables
3️⃣ Reference to those variables

Lexical Scope (VERY IMPORTANT)

JavaScript follows lexical scoping.
Meaning:
👉 Scope depends on where the function is written, NOT where it is called.
Closures work because of lexical scope.
If interviewer asks:
“Why do closures exist?”
Answer:
Because JavaScript uses lexical scoping.
Strong answer.

Lifecycle Explanation (interview-level)

Normally:
function finishes → local variables destroyed
BUT…
If an inner function still references those variables:
JavaScript keeps them alive in memory
This preservation of variables = closure.
