🪄 Simple Example
function outer() {
  let count = 0;
  function inner() {
    count++;
    console.log(count);
  }

  return inner;
}

const fn = outer();

fn(); // 1
fn(); // 2
fn(); // 3

🤯 Why does this work?
Normally:

outer() runs

finishes

local variables should disappear

BUT…

inner() still remembers count.

That remembering = closure

🎯 Core idea
Closure = function + its lexical environment

Not just the function.

Also includes:

variables
parameters
outer scope references


Bundled together.

