Means:

👉 scope based on where code is WRITTEN, not where executed.

JavaScript uses lexical scoping.

🔥 Real Interview Explanation (simple words)

When a function is created:

✔ it stores reference to variables around it
✔ not values — references
Even if outer function finishes, variables stay alive if inner function uses them.
🏗 Step-by-step memory view

For this:

function outer(){
  let x = 10;
  return function(){
    console.log(x);
  }
}
JS internally keeps:
inner → reference → outer scope → x = 10
So x never dies.
